.TH GOLEM-CHECK 1 2026-11-09 golem-team-shield "User Commands"
.SH NAME
golem-check \- grade how the AI coding agents on this machine are set up
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B golem-check
.RB [ \-json " [" \-with\-score "] | " \-sarif " | " \-html ]
.RB [ \-repo
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-fail\-on
.IR severity ]
.br
.B golem-check \-repo
.I dir
.B \-upload
.RB [ \-golem\-url
.IR url ]
.RB [ \-upload\-repo
.IR owner/name ]
.RB [ \-upload\-commit
.IR sha ]
.RB [ \-upload\-ref
.IR ref ]
.br
.B golem-check \-fix
.RB [ \-yes ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.br
.B golem-check \-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B golem-check
reads the settings of the AI coding agents on this machine, grades them out of
100, scores each area, and prints what to fix, worst first.
The areas are run modes (permission prompts off, every command allowed, every
folder trusted), permissions (allow rules for any command, file, page or MCP
tool), MCP servers (how each is started and reached, and what the tool
descriptions an agent stored tell it), instructions and skills
(text that tells the agent to hide things, send secrets out, or ignore its
instructions), hooks, secrets written into settings, plugin sources, and
protections (which agents run their commands in a sandbox or the Golem jail).
Where the jail is required
.RB ( require_jail
in the managed settings or
.IR ~/.golem/shield.json ,
or the Golem agent's workspace), an agent whose commands run outside it is
dangerous, and
.B "\-fail\-on dangerous"
fails on it.
.PP
It reads Claude Code (including its managed settings and MDM profiles),
Claude Desktop, Cursor, Codex, Gemini CLI, Copilot CLI, Devin, Hermes,
OpenClaw, and the VS Code family of editors (VS Code, VS Code Insiders,
VSCodium, Cursor, Windsurf, Antigravity), and the settings of each repository
Claude Code has opened.
Copilot CLI's run modes are command-line flags no file records; for it,
.B golem-check
reads the MCP servers, a repository's
.I .github/copilot-instructions.md
and the shell jail.
.PP
Unless it is run with
.BR "\-fix \-yes" ,
it reads files and nothing else.
Unless it is run with
.BR \-upload ,
it sends nothing anywhere.
It never starts an MCP server.
It reads the tools a server describes from the copy an agent wrote down the
last time it started the server (Cursor, Antigravity, the VS Code family and
Codex keep one), and lists each configured server no agent kept a copy of as
not read.
A finding in a tool description names the server, the tool and the rule,
never the text.
It names a secret's file and setting, never the secret.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-json
Print every finding, informational ones included, as JSON: a list of objects
with
.BR client ,
.BR area ,
.BR check ,
.B severity
.RB ( dangerous ,
.B suspicious
or
.BR info ),
.BR scope ,
.BR location ,
.BR setting ,
.BR value ,
.BR detail ,
and where they apply
.B subject
(the server, hook event, skill or plugin) and
.BR line .
.TP
.B \-with\-score
With
.BR \-json ,
print an object holding the grade and each area's score
.RB ( score )
beside the findings
.RB ( items ).
.TP
.B \-sarif
Print the findings as SARIF 2.1.0, for GitHub code scanning.
.TP
.B \-html
Print the report as one self-contained HTML page, with no scripts and nothing
fetched, to hand to a security team.
It names no user or host.
.TP
.BI \-repo " dir"
Check only the agent files the repository at
.I dir
carries, and nothing from the home directory: what a clone hands everyone who
works in it.
Locations are relative to
.IR dir .
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the report to
.I file
instead of standard output.
.TP
.BI \-fail\-on " severity"
Exit 3 when a finding is at least
.I severity
.RB ( suspicious
or
.BR dangerous );
.B none
(the default) never does.
.TP
.B \-fix
Print, as a diff per file, the changes to your own agent settings that would
undo each dangerous or suspicious finding, and change nothing.
A run mode that stops an agent asking (Claude Code's
.B bypassPermissions
default and accepted bypass warning, Cursor CLI's
.B unrestricted
approval mode, Codex's
.BR danger\-full\-access )
and an allow rule for any command, file, page or MCP tool are taken out, so the
agent goes back to its default, which asks.
.B enableAllProjectMcpServers
and an editor's
.B chat.tools.global.autoApprove
are taken out; workspace trust and Gemini CLI's folder trust are turned back on.
A settings or sign-in file holding a credential that other users can read
becomes readable by its owner only, and a hook script other users can change
loses their write permission.
It never changes a repository's files, managed or MDM policy, a file with
comments or one that does not parse, a symbolic link or a folder; it deletes
nothing and moves no secret.
The plan ends with every dangerous or suspicious finding it left alone, the ones
it has no change for included, and where to change each by hand.
It takes only
.B \-yes
and
.BR \-o .
.TP
.B \-yes
With
.BR \-fix ,
make the changes.
Each file whose contents change is first copied into
.IR ~/.golem/check\-backups/<time>/ ,
and the check then scans again.
If the score or the grade went down, a new dangerous or suspicious finding
appeared, or a changed file no longer reads back, every file is put back and
the exit status is 4.
A file that changed after the plan was made stops it before anything is
written.
.TP
.B \-upload
With
.BR \-repo ,
also send the findings to Golem, where the console shows them beside each
machine's agent settings, marked as from a repository and CI.
It needs a CI token minted in the Golem console (IAM, AI agents, New CI token)
in
.BR GOLEM_CHECK_TOKEN .
It sends each finding's client, check, severity, file, setting and detail, never
a file's contents, with the repository's name, commit and ref.
Each send replaces what the repository sent before, so send from one branch.
It prints one line to standard error saying what was sent, or why nothing was;
a failure does not change the exit status.
.TP
.BI \-golem\-url " url"
Where
.B \-upload
sends: https, or http on this machine only.
By default
.BR GOLEM_URL ,
or Golem's hosted service, https://relay.golem.team.
.TP
.BI \-upload\-repo " owner/name"
The repository
.B \-upload
names.
By default
.B GITHUB_REPOSITORY
or GitLab CI's
.BR CI_PROJECT_PATH .
.TP
.BI \-upload\-commit " sha"
The full SHA of the commit checked.
By default
.B GITHUB_SHA
or
.BR CI_COMMIT_SHA .
.TP
.BI \-upload\-ref " ref"
The ref checked.
By default
.BR GITHUB_REF ,
or GitLab CI's branch or tag.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GOLEM_CHECK_TOKEN
The CI token
.B \-upload
sends with, as the console showed it.
It is read from the environment only, never from a flag, and never printed.
It can report a repository's findings and do nothing else.
.TP
.B GOLEM_URL
Where
.B \-upload
sends, when
.B \-golem\-url
is not given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the report was written, whatever it found, or when
.B \-fix
made or planned its changes; 1 when it could not write the report or a file; 2
for a usage error; 3 when
.B \-fail\-on
found something at least that severe; 4 when
.B "\-fix \-yes"
put every file back because the result was worse.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.golem/check\-backups/<time>/
The files
.B "\-fix \-yes"
changed, as they were, under their paths relative to the home directory, and
.IR manifest.json ,
which lists each file's path and permissions.
Owner-only.
A jailed agent can neither read nor write
.IR ~/.golem .
.SH GITHUB ACTIONS
The repository is also a GitHub Action that runs
.B golem-check \-repo
on the checked-out repository, uploads the SARIF report to code scanning, and
fails the job on a finding at least as severe as its
.B fail-on
input:
.PP
.nf
  - uses: GolemTeam/golem-team-shield@v0.2.0
    with:
      fail-on: dangerous
.fi
.PP
With
.B "upload: true"
and
.B "golem-token: ${{ secrets.GOLEM_CHECK_TOKEN }}"
it also sends the findings to Golem, from the repository's default branch only.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR golem\-exec (1)
.PP
https://github.com/GolemTeam/golem-team-shield
